\f1\fs22 St Hedges Cathedral singularly proved that the thinking processes of the Airborne didn\'92t always draw the straightest line between the itch of greed and the quick return of profit. That is, if you ignore the more egregious attempts of the Country Party to pork-barrel taxes away from the cities and straight into the hands of mining magnates.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The cathedral and its environs was undoubtedly the costliest capital expenditure project in the history of the Great Meritocracy of Ulyssia. Ostentatious, stately, awe-inspiring, and a triumph of engineering\'97these were just some of superlatives used to describe the cathedral\'92s bathrooms.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The cathedral itself existed on another plane of grandeur altogether. So too the grounds, which nestled in a nine-sided shallow bowl-shaped platform a hundred metres above the Layer. It covered more than 10 hectares in area, more than 1 billion square centimetres, because the venture capitalists who sat on the board of St Hedges Permanent Holdings and Stranglings LLC demanded at least one measurement of its grandiosity should exceed 1 billion somethings. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At each of the nine corners of the platform, a statue of one of the Nine Major Saints of Latter Day Capitalism looked out, valiant and imposing, over the sun-drenched cerulean skies. They saw all who came and went, each guarding a hover-pad stuck to the platform like huge, grey, stale lollipops.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At the centre of the platform, St Hedges presided over spectacular sweeping lawns and beds of flowers framed by topiaries of geometrically trimmed hedges and imposing copses of trees. The flower beds were of all the most extraordinary colours of the rainbow except red, which was, of course, an unwelcome colour, representing bad news in politics, or on any financial report. Following the same construct of good and evil, the lawns were all black, representing perennial profit, and the death of socialism.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Black grass, you say? True! Horticulturalists were hired to cultivate a new breed of grass, which was aptly called Accountancy Black Buffalo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Wide, sweeping stone paths curved through the grounds and converged on the cathedral, which both inspired and conquered the fiscal souls of all who approached. Incorporating advanced metallurgy for the structure, and brutalist masonry for the facades of limestone and bluestone (the local sandstone having been discarded for its tendency to age from virtuous yellow gold to shitty brown), it was its clever use of glass ceilings which invariably struck a first-time visitor dumb, reminding them of the limitlessness of the skies, both literally and in the metaphorical opportunistic sense.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Everything about the cathedral and its environs was designed to impress. No expense in the construction and detailing of the cathedral was spared because every Airborne knew, in the warm cockles of their offshore accounts, an impressed person was a distracted person, and a distracted person was easier to rob.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The floors of the nave were paved in flawless marble, the pews hewn from endangered hardwoods, their cushions reportedly made of genetically engineered mink or seal\'97both farmed species bred square and cushion-shaped for convenience. Intricate stained-glass windows depicted minor prophets of Latter Day Capitalism doling out enshittified social network subscriptions to the huddled masses. Adorning the walls were graphs and tables inlaid with precious jewels and metals that glinted and coruscated as they caught light from the windows and the bling of congregations. If the light was right, the trend lines of key holy indicators such as customer acquisition cost, customer lifetime value, recurring revenue, churn rate, and net promoter score seemed to leap off the walls and come alive.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The seating capacity of the nave is Commercial in Confidence, but has been estimated to be somewhere between 3000 and 23 billion. No one has ever bothered to count the seats, but the St Hedges Cathedral Preservation Society (incorporated on Kangaroo Island) consistently boasts a seating capacity growth rate of 23% year-on-year. No one actually believes this, but the mere publication of the numbers seems sufficient to ward off disgruntled investors and hostile take-overs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Whether the cathedral seated 3000 or 23 billion, the congregation attending the holy joint venture of Alexander Shakti and Sarah Jacobson were too few to suffocate all the plump, square platypus cushions (or were they otter? ), even as their arse cheeks were privilege-flabby. Initially, Alexander\'92s side of the aisle made a better show of it, but that was mainly due to the number of production crews working every angle of the ceremony, including an in-depth piece about the Accountancy Black lawns, and whether they really were just painted that colour.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s side of the nave was barely one third full. That is, until the Elvi showed up.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They descended from the sky in waves\'97parachutes blooming against the cerulean blue like a fleet of jewelled jellyfish. Dozens of them, perhaps hundreds, drifting down toward the Accountancy Black lawns with the unhurried precision of people who\'92d done this before.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Security sprinted across the grounds, but the Elvi had already gathered their chutes and were striding toward the cathedral, bundles of light fabric slung over jumpsuits. High in the distance, the faint drone of engines receded. No one could quite agree on what kind of aircraft they\'92d seen\'97old, certainly. Fixed wings, possibly. But by the time anyone thought to raise an alarm, the Elvi were already inside, adjusting their sunglasses. Despite having just descended from the sky onto one of Ulyssia\'92s most exclusive venues, all their hair was perfect.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The younger Airborne attendees, being fans of the overnight success of Elvi music, were enraptured by the mass appearance of their cultural heroes. Cries of, \'93We love you, Elvi\'94, \'93I want to have your babies\'94, and \'93Sing
\f2\i Evolve Me Tender
\f1\i0 \'94 disrupted the decorum of the solemn mood. Older, more conservative Airborne would have nothing of such an offensive intrusion by lazy, low-class minimum-wage-abusers, and with dismissive waves of angry protest, filed out of the pews towards the exits.
\f2\i \

\f1\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander panicked, his moment draining away. He flashed his signature gormless confusion at his senior associate producer, who decoded the plea instantly:
\f2\i For tax sake, fix this!
\f1\i0  Cameras swivelled toward the exits like weapon turrets. The fleeing Airborne, faced with probable unflattering coverage, reconsidered. What a shame to miss the reception. Most returned.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi continued to file in until all remaining seat cushions were flattened, and the event became standing room only, which is to say the marketing department could plausibly claim the crowd numbered more than 23 billion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Piotr Dunhilton had tried three separate excuses to avoid attending. A critical board meeting (Eloise rescheduled it). A sudden onset of food poisoning (Eloise had sent her personal physician to his office with a knowing smile and a bottle of antacids).\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When Eloise Shakti invited you to her son\'92s wedding, you attended. Even if\'97especially if\'97the bride was the Holy Momma of an emerging Elvi religion that you\'92d watched being born on grainy phone footage while throwing axes at defenceless animals.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>So here he sat, second row, jaw clenched, watching the cathedral fill with both Airborne elite and an alarming number of sequinned jumpsuits.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He\'92d commanded rooms his entire career. Boardrooms, situation rooms, the plunge pool at the Residence where subordinates stood in suits while he floated. But this cathedral wasn\'92t his, and you couldn\'92t fire a hymn.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>With apoplexy, the Very Arch Bishop of Business of Ulyssia, Ignatius d\'92Molesto, watched the mob sweating in his cathedral, and lamented the inadequacy of the security he had bought for the event. With resignation, he huffed and sighed from his very comfortable, well-cushioned throne.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis entered the nave to cheers from his flock, strode up the aisle and stood before the Very Arch Bishop. They regarded each other and, when the High Elvis realised the Bishop would not vacate the throne for him, he turned and stood proudly at the right side.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At precisely that moment, sunlight poured through the stained glass windows and fell just so, reflecting off the precious jewels and metals in all the graphs and tables etched into the walls of the nave. By an incalculable accident of geometry, much of the reflected light struck pinpoints in the sunglasses and jewellery of the High Elvis, and from there shone on the congregation with the intensity of a thousand mirror balls in a thousand nightclubs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi gazed upon the spectacle with awe and amazement, and knew beyond a certainty it was a sign. (Just what kind of sign it was, they would have to ask the Great Teacher later, as he had declined to attend the ceremony.) As one, they took to their feet, enfolded their arms in the manner of cradling a newborn baby, and chanted, \'93Holy Momma! Holy Momma!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton became all the more alarmed as the chanting rang out and the very pregnant Sarah appeared at the back of the cathedral, a black veil drawn over her face like a closing statement. She smiled with embarrassment, though with the veil it was hard to tell whether the smile was for the congregation or herself. Of all the hills to die on, she\'92d chosen one with a pulpit. She wasn\'92t rejecting the title. She was accepting it\'97publicly, officially. What he\'92d seen on that shaky video in Hatchet & Grimes was no longer some cult gathering in a converted brothel. It was here, now, being sanctified in the Very Arch Bishop\'92s cathedral with Airborne witnesses.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah Jacobson wasn\'92t just the Holy Momma to a mob in the Ghetto anymore. She was the Holy Momma at her own Airborne wedding.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The earth turned and rolled along its orbit, the sunbeams tilted, and the High Elvis was no longer cast as a being of light. He stepped forward and waved the Elvi chanting down. When reverent silence had been restored, the Very Arch Bishop rose and pompously strode to the pulpit to begin the ceremony, taking the utmost care that his robes swished perfectly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis pinched his seat.
